#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform

#% text
# `inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform(c)` gives the data from the continuous wavelet data c; `inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform(c, w, sel)` the part of the data carried by the selected scales.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Every scale is a convolution, so the least-squares inverse is a ratio in each Fourier coefficient: x_k = sum_s Psi_s(w_k) W_s(w_k)/sum_s |Psi_s(w_k)|^2. The frequencies that the scales hardly reach (below 1e-4 of the largest sum, the constant part among them) are kept in the data object, so the whole inverse reproduces the data to rounding. With a selection the kept part is left out.

#% item
# w must be the wavelet of the transform (or `automatic`). The option `method: "LeastSquares"` names this method, the only one.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
x = n(table(sin(k/5) + sin(k), [k, 100]))

#% cell
c = continuous_wavelet_transform(x, gabor_wavelet(), [6, 4])

#% cell
norm(inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform(c) - x) < 1e-10
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The coarse octaves alone keep the slow oscillation:

#% cell
list_line_plot(inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform(c, automatic, [5 | 6, _]))
#% output
#= image("inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform.files/inverse_continuous_wavelet_transform-4.svgz")

